# This repository checks the bytes as they are checked out.
#
# `analysis/scripts/verify_threshold_freeze.py` and `verify_data_hashes.py` recompute the git blob
# identifier — sha1("blob <size>\0" + content) — from files in the working tree and compare it with
# the blob at the corresponding upstream commit. If a checkout rewrites line endings, the identifier
# changes even though the content is the same, and the verification fails.
#
# GitHub's `windows-latest` runner defaults to `core.autocrlf = true`. A machine configured with
# `core.autocrlf = input` never converts on checkout, so **this class of failure cannot be observed
# there at all**; it has to be simulated deliberately.
#
# Attribute rules are last-match-wins, so the order below is: general rules first, then the
# exception for frozen raw evidence.

# --- General: keep tracked text at LF ---
*.py        text eol=lf
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf
*.cff       text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf

# Files without an extension, which the rules above do not reach.
SEED        text eol=lf
LICENSE     text eol=lf
LICENSE-MIT text eol=lf

# --- Exception: git must not touch frozen raw evidence at all ---
#
# On 2026-09-12, a global `core.autocrlf = input` stripped the carriage returns from
# `data/raw/es3-verdict-forensic.json` the moment it was first added, so the committed content no
# longer matched the original (11,743 -> 11,401 bytes). `data/data.md` pins the original SHA-256, so
# a normalised copy would have made the recorded digest disagree with the shipped file — which is
# exactly what recording a digest is meant to prevent. The working tree still held the original
# bytes, so nothing surfaced until a checkout replaced them.
data/raw/** -text
env/uv.lock -text
